# Dimora product vision

Dimora is a Rust game engine built around a unified spatial model for 2.5D game development, combining
powerful programming APIs with purpose-built visual tools, including a user-friendly editor. Central
principle: 2.5D is a spatial world, not a stack of 2D layers. Sprites, tilemaps, 2D rigs, 3D meshes and 3D
characters share transforms, camera, depth and resource infrastructure. They do not need to share one
implementation. The editor is a core goal, not an optional extra.

Full text lives in the docs; do not restate it here or in code:

- Vision, principles, non-goals: `docs/VISION.md`
- What exists today versus planned: `docs/CAPABILITIES.md`
- Milestone order: `ROADMAP.md`; current milestone and tasks: `TASKS.md`

## Working rules

1. Before changing anything, read `TASKS.md` (current milestone) and the relevant rows of
   `docs/CAPABILITIES.md`, and inspect the existing code. Reuse and extend existing systems.
2. Implement only the current approved milestone and task. Do not start the next milestone, and do not add a
   feature, crate or dependency "for later". If the task seems to need something outside the milestone, stop and
   record it in `TASKS.md` instead.
3. Prefer a small end-to-end version with a test and a runnable example over a general abstraction. Extract a
   shared abstraction only when two real implementations show the same shape.
4. Design for the editor now: components are plain, named, serializable data, changes go through commands that
   can be undone, assets and entities have stable ids. Build no feature that hides state an editor must edit,
   and no feature that works only in the editor. Do not build the editor ahead of its milestone or its
   stability gate (ADR-0003); small read-only developer tools (logging, diagnostics, inspection, debug draw,
   asset validation) are delivered in the milestones named in the roadmap's "Incremental developer tooling".
5. Dimora is not defined by one camera or look. Perspective, orthographic, isometric, top-down, side-scrolling
   and custom views are configurations of one camera model over one world (`docs/adr/0004-*`). Do not add a
   renderer, mode or world model per view, do not assume one projection or ground plane in new code, and keep
   projection, gameplay orientation and visual style separate. Pixel-art and fog features are optional style
   policies, not foundations.
6. Position in the world decides visibility, occlusion and parallax. Do not add integer layers or z-indices
   where a position could express the same thing.
7. Gameplay does not know about its visual representation, and scripts and tools do not know about renderer
   internals.
8. Respect the non-goals in `docs/VISION.md` (no general-purpose 3D engine, no Unity or Godot clone, no
   physics solver, no networking, no second script language). The editor is a goal, not a non-goal. Going against one needs an ADR with a concrete
   need.
9. Status must be honest. Keep four things distinct in docs: confirmed owner decisions, recommended but
   unapproved decisions, implemented capabilities, and future requirements (`docs/DECISIONS.md`, "Owner
   decisions"). Never describe an unapproved proposal as confirmed. The current licence is the proprietary notice in `LICENSE` (OD-3); a future model is a separate owner decision. Never mark something done because a type or placeholder exists; "done" needs a
   passing test or a recorded run. Update the status rows in the same commit that changes behaviour.
